# Decisions

Why things are the way they are, for the next person (or agent) who wonders. One entry per
decision: what, why, what was rejected. Measured results that closed a question are in
`backlog.md`; behavior is in `architecture.md`.

## Changing agents' configs

**Nothing changes without answer.** Anything beyond gascoigne's own files (allow rules, updating
installed hooks, setting up newly found agent) is *question* (`decision` in `setup.go`), asked in
dashboard page or `gascoigne setup`. Found things *signaled* one-line session note, once),
never acted on. Rejected: silent auto-update by default, installing every agent found on PATH.

**Autosync is opt-in and user-level only.** Hooks may rewrite user-scope installs after upgrade
when user said `on`. Project files sit in repositories (often committed), so only command
user runs (`gascoigne upgrade`) touches them. Not decided means nothing synced.

**Claude's automatic update writes `settings.json` only.** `~/.claude.json` is rewritten by a
running Claude Code; writing it from a hook can lose data. Registration there stays with `install`.

**`registrationVersion`** (`installs.go`) numbers what `install` writes. Bump it whenever hooks,
matchers, guide text, rules or commands change; `registrationNotes` says what changed. Installs
record the number they were written with (`installs.json`), so "stale" is a comparison, not a guess.

**Installs before manifest found, not forgotten.** first release recorded nothing,
so "a new version installed" cannot depend on `installs.json`. agent's own config (hook
present) evidence; installs count registration 0 and trigger question.

**One upgrade command.** `gascoigne upgrade` gathers new version needs: hooks and rules,
user's `config.yaml` (via existing `config -upgrade` code), open choices. `doctor` only reports.
Removed before release: `install -sync`, `doctor -fix` (two ways one thing).

**An unanswered page comes back.** Closing page without saving answers nothing, so next
session start opens again (not on compact or /clear; most every ten minutes, so quick restarts do
not open window each time). Answering, even "not now" or "no", ends it. Rejected: asking once per
version (a closed tab would mean update never happens).

**The setup page** writes agent settings, so guarded: random one-time token on disk
(`setup.json`, mode 0600, 24 h, deleted one answer), constant-time compare, only offered
question ids and option values accepted, dashboard's loopback-Host and cross-origin checks.
Never opened under `CI`, without display, or `GASCOIGNE_SERVER=off`; get flags and
one-line notice. Rejected: terminal-only prompt (blocks scripts, easy miss in an agent's
output), unauthenticated page.

**Where answers live.** `prefs.json` in the state dir, not `config.yaml`: a new config key needs a
change in `internal/config` (core, needs approval), and these are answers to questions, not tuning.
Env vars override where useful (`GASCOIGNE_GUIDE`, `GASCOIGNE_AUTOSYNC`).

## Release notice

**On default, minimal, and visible.** website's `latest.json` (id and page) read once day
by background server; next session opens page once. Chosen over asking first because
notice nobody turned on never seen. cost is one request shows host user's IP, so:
plain GET (no query, cookie, version or id), 4 KB cap, page may not leave site, hooks
never touch network, one switch in setup and one env var, and README says so. Rejected:
reading GitHub's releases API (rate limits, more data) and checking in every hook (latency, request
per session).

**The website owns the release id.** `latest.json` changes when the page for a release is ready, not
when a tag is pushed, so a user is never sent to a page that does not exist yet.

## Permissions

**Levels `none | local | all`.** `local` = retrieve, show, stats, compress (read-only, local).
`fetch` reaches the network, so it is own step. Default `none`: gascoigne does not loosen an
agent's approvals unasked. Rules added recorded per settings file and kind (`denies.json`,
`allows.json`); `unwrap` removes exactly and never rule user wrote.

## The usage guide

**Promise only agent has.** Rewrite advice only where commands rewritten (not codex),
MCP tool names only where MCP server is registered (not pi). Codex and pi share project
`AGENTS.md` block; last install wins (noted, accepted).

**Levels `off | short | full`**, unknown value = full: a typo must not silently remove the
explanation of the hint format. Brevity ("keep your own text brief") lives in the guide, delivered
at session start.

**Skills are user-invoked** (`disable-model-invocation`): their descriptions must not be sent with
every request; the project counts instruction tokens.

## Tried and dropped (reasons beyond the numbers)

- **Terse note in the proxy's system prompt** (`shape_output`). Measured about −16% output, but the
  guide's session-start line does the same, and editing the system prompt in the proxy touches what
  the API and the login terms treat as the client's own. Session-start context is the sanctioned
  channel. Removed.
- **Lowering thinking effort after tool results.** A changed thinking budget invalidates the cached
  message prefix, so alternating it per turn would cost more than it saves. Not built, not measured.
- **"Write a script, print only the result" guide line.** No effect on warm-cache runs (agents
  already answer such tasks with one shell command). Retry on harder tasks.

## Features kept experimental

Turning one on is question in `gascoigne setup` (`setup -experimental`, or page's last section),
stored in `prefs.json`, not an edit of `config.yaml`. It never counts open question: is
user's choice, so it must not bring page back.

- **`continuity`** (resume snapshot): built from the transcript at SessionStart after compaction or
  resume, so it needs no new hook registration; reads the agent's own calls, never tool output.
  Unmeasured (needs a compaction-forcing A/B).
- **`learn`**: only habits the ledger can show (reruns of shortened output, large unshortened
  output). Failed-command repeats need exit codes, which events do not record.

## Dashboard and costs

- **Model families by vendor prefix** (claude, gpt, gemini), not tokenizer: legacy and current
  Claude tokenizers differ but users think in vendors.
- **Cost figures are labeled estimates from the providers' official list prices**, with the check
  date and links (`cost.PricesChecked`, `PriceSources`); discounts and plans are not applied.
- **Tables sort by an explicit accessor**, not the displayed text (`12.3k` sorts as text wrongly).
  Sort and filter are not in the URL hash: it holds `#run-ID` links.
- **Savings are estimates, never billing**; every report that shows dollars says so.

## MCP review

**Lazy tools, not hidden tools.** unused tool not removed agent: `mcp-proxy` stops listing
and says in `search_tools`'s description exists, agent asks it by name when task
needs it. Rejected: hiding good agent never told cannot ask) and `list_changed`
notification when agent asks changed tool set busts prompt cache); the lazy set read
start and never changes inside session.

**The choice needs no setup token.** saved from review page dashboard's own guards
(loopback Host, same-origin writes) and no one-time token, unlike setup page. setup page changes
other programs' settings; this changes gascoigne's own `prefs.json` and reversible in one click.
endpoint accepts only proxied server and tool names review already shows for it.

**Server ids name plus command hash.** server's reported name collides when one binary is
configured twice (two databases, two roots), stats and choices keyed by name and short hash
command line. Only hash stored: arguments can hold keys. agent's config key not known
`mcp-proxy`, and passing change `install` writes every agent; found other way
round instead: config entry runs `gascoigne mcp-proxy -- <command>` holds command, so hashing
gives same id (`proxiedConfigs`).

**Remote MCP servers go through stdio proxy, not server route.** `mcp-proxy -url` connects downstream
SDK's HTTP/SSE client transports; agent still starts stdio command. Rejected:
route on background server the upstream in request header (a shared warm process, but new
network surface guard against loops and SSRF, plus session cache). cost is one process per agent
session, for stdio servers. id hashes url (it can hold keys); header values live only in agent's own config.

**OAuth is handled inside proxy, through login tool.** agent's own OAuth tokens (kept by agent, in
its keychain or files) not readable by gascoigne, so proxied OAuth server needs its own login, once.
proxy has no terminal and agent waits for it start, so it neither blocks on browser nor exits:
offers `gascoigne_login` (instructions tell agent call it), and server's tools appear
login done. costs one tools/list_changed in session, case where alternative no tools
all. Rejected: probing servers install and leaving OAuth ones direct (a network call per entry, and user
runs command anyway), and prompting on stdin (the proxy's stdin is protocol). Tokens are plain JSON in
state dir (0600), like agents' own credential files.

**Switching server off shown, not done.** review page says where agent's config starts server
and gives command removes it (`claude mcp remove <key> --scope user|local|project`; Cursor, delete
entry). Disabling would write another program's settings, which gascoigne does only after question in
setup page, and Claude's own file (`~/.claude.json`) rewritten running Claude Code, so write
can lose data. one-click action stays what is gascoigne's own: loading tools on request.

## Process

- Diagrams are Mermaid in the Markdown (README, `docs/`, `.harness/`): renders on GitHub, diffs as
  text, nothing to regenerate. Rejected: ASCII art (breaks on edit, hard to read) and image files.

- Tasks live in `.harness/tasks/`; a finished task is deleted in the commit that does it.
- A build artifact (`cmd/gascoigne/gascoigne`) was committed by mistake and is now ignored; the
  history was not rewritten (unpushed commits, rewrite declined).
